  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate Adaptive Livelihood Security of Sundarbans Resource Dependent Communities</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The southwest coastal Bangladesh Sundarbans mangrove ecosystem of Khulna, Bagerhat and Satkhira districts is a high risk zone due to sea level rise caused by global climate change. Millions of coastal people are vulnerable to the inundation of water, especially vulnerable are the livelihoods of the Sundarbans forest dependant people. CCEC aims to reach 1,900 people (940 men and 960 women) from 380 vulnerable Sundarbans resource harvester families (those who collect resources from the Sundarbans forest such as honey or palms) across Mongla Upazila under Bagerhat District and Dacope Upazila under Khulna District. 200 Sundarbans resources harvesters (including crab, fish, Nypa - a palm tree collected for thatching and honey collectors) will form eight livelihood groups and two Upazila associations. These groups will receive livelihood specific inputs and training on topics including Disaster Risk Reduction and climate change adaptation. 200 Sundarbans resource harvester&apos;s wives and 20 tiger widows (women whose husbands were killed by tigers) will also receive training on agricultural, fisheries, and livestock in the context of climate adaptive livelihoods. 20 tiger widows will receive handicraft training and the project will establish a link with the Bangladesh Tourism Corporation to promote the sale of their products. In coordination with Bangladesh Legal Aid Services Trust (BLAST), the project will provide legal aid to 100 project participant families who face discrimination in accessing resources. The project will also provide livelihood support for 200 people, to promote climate adaptive livelihoods. 60 people will receive cash-for-work during the lean period to strengthen year-round food security.</narrative>
  </description>
